  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Infrastructure</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia is supporting infrastructure workforce development in Vanuatu through the provision of targeted, flexible skills training, complemented by enterprise start-up support. In 2024, our Vanuatu Skills Partnership, through its network of provincial Skills Centres, worked with local registered training providers to deliver an accredited Certificate II qualification in Building and Construction in remote West Santo and a Certificate II qualification in Plumbing in Malekula. These &#x2018;off campus&#x2019; trainings enabled inclusive participation, including women and people with disabilities, and were integrated with community infrastructure projects to strengthen practical experience. In 2024, additional financial literacy and governance workshops, alongside business registration support, were also delivered to the new graduates. As a result, seven new businesses supported by Skills Centres became operational, providing employment for 51 tradespeople (44 men, 2 with disabilities and 7 women). These businesses have now successfully secured contracts in Santo and Malekula, generating local employment and contributing to infrastructure development.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In 2022, the Vanuatu Skills Partnership made important contributions in support of the national architecture for skills development in Vanuatu, specifically through its support for the development of a National Skills Policy and the convening of the annual Post-School Education and Training conference. This work is directly supporting national and provincial stakeholders to strengthen their capacity to coordinate and manage the skills system in Vanuatu.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic policy reform and management and private sector development</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In 2024, Australia&#x2019;s Vanuatu Skills Partnership (VSP) supported improved market access for rural producers through 31 local organisations, including nine farmer cooperatives or associations, four provincial handicraft hubs and 18 craft producer associations. VSP delivered coordinated trade events, coaching in marketing, and value addition activities. VSP supported training in pruning and curing for vanilla farmers to improve the quality of their vanilla, with 29% of the 2024 crop rated as &#x2018;Grade A&#x2019;. Pepper farmers in Malo improved sales through new packaging and health-focused marketing. Handicraft producers from provincial hubs increased sales and market links at the &#x2018;Vanuatu Made&#x2019; Trade Show in Port Vila. The Tafea province hub manager participated in a New Zealand trade event securing international export orders for the first time. Local trade festivals in Torba province and on Ambae island further boosted domestic sales for local weavers, particularly women, and are now being planned as annual events.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic empowerment</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In 2024, Vanuatu Skills Partnership (VSP) strengthened its commitment to women&#x2019;s economic empowerment. Women&apos;s participation in skills training reached 53%, up from 50% in 2023 and 40% in 2022, with increased involvement in traditionally male-dominated fields such as plumbing and construction. 419 women, including 23 women with disabilities, participated in inclusive skills development activities across key industries to support their professional and entrepreneurial goals. This included the provision of customised financial and business management coaching to 72 women entrepreneurs including six women with disabilities, to help them build sustainable enter-prises and cooperatives. Tailored support mechanisms, such as flexible training schedules, transport and childcare assistance facilitated increased participation of women in skills activities.  Concurrent support to four provincial &#x2018;handicraft hubs&#x2019; further enabled access to domestic and international markets for women craft entrepreneurs in rural areas. Collectively, these results demonstrate VSP&apos;s ongoing contributions to women&#x2019;s economic empowerment in Vanuatu.&#x200d;

 &#x201c;Through the support of VSP, I completed Certificates I &amp; II in Building Construction qualifications delivered flexibly on Tanna by nationally recognised training providers. Ongoing business coaching then enabled me to win a government tender to build the Central Tanna Area Council office. Today, my company employs eight local people.&#x201d; Mary Iawilu</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Food security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Vanuatu Skills Partnership through its Farm to School and Farm to Hospital program is providing training and mentoring to farmers and linking them to local hospitals and schools. This is providing locally grown, sustainable ingredients for more nutritious meals for patients and students and reducing reliance on expensive imported foods. In 2022, the program trained 3 staff at the Northern Provincial Hospital and 44 farmers (14 women) at Ambaebulu School in Penama.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
